---
/**
 * /guides/exp-then-vs-now - the "which v83 EXP numbers still apply?"
 * reference. Covers mob EXP, quest EXP, and the party EXP formula
 * with an honest "here's what we know / don't know" framing.
 *
 * SEO target: 'maplestory classic exp changes', 'MSCW mob exp
 * different from v83', 'maplestory classic quest exp', 'maplestory
 * classic leveling different'. Returning-player intent query.
 *
 * All CoT 2 mob values below come directly from mobs.json
 * (stats.exp). v83 baseline values are from Hidden Street / OldSchool
 * wikis - we cite the range of values seen across community sources
 * rather than pretending there's ONE canonical v83 number (there
 * often isn't - GMS retuned during patches and private servers
 * added their own multipliers).
 */

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Then vs Now: EXP & Progression Changes (v83 to CoT 2) - Liven's Classic Maple Guide"
	description="The v83 wiki mob-EXP tables are mostly still accurate for MapleStory Classic World, but there are shifts worth knowing about. Datamine-verified CoT 2 numbers for iconic mobs, quest EXP examples, and an honest note on party EXP formula uncertainty."
	currentPath="/guides"
	theme="ellinia"
	decorations={false}
>
	<section class="section-heading">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow eyebrow--guide">CoT 2 vs v83 Reality Check</span>
			<h1>Then vs Now: EXP & Progression</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Old v83 mob-EXP tables are the returning player's default mental
				model. Are they still accurate for Classic World? Mostly - but
				there are shifts, some of them significant, and we don't have
				live-server confirmation on the party EXP formula at all yet.
				This guide walks through what we know, what we don't, and how
				to fact-check any specific value.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose">
		<div class="callout callout--framing">
			<strong>Data sources:</strong> all "CoT 2 datamine" numbers below
			are pulled directly from <code>mobs.json</code> and
			<code>quests.json</code>, extracted from the Closed Online Test 2
			client. "v83 baseline" references community wikis (Hidden Street,
			OldSchool) - which themselves disagree on some values because GMS
			retuned during its own patch history. When we cite a v83 number,
			assume &plusmn;20% variance across sources unless we explicitly
			say otherwise.
		</div>

		<h2 id="mob-exp-comparison">Mob EXP: mostly the same, with exceptions</h2>
		<p>
			The datamine surprises here. Most iconic v83 mob EXP values
			carried over into CoT 2 unchanged. Selective shifts DO exist -
			the low-level Slime notably almost doubled - but the "everything
			has been retuned!" narrative you might have heard is largely
			false. Concrete numbers:
		</p>

		<div class="table-wrap">
		<table class="exp-table">
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Mob</th>
					<th>Lv</th>
					<th>CoT 2 EXP</th>
					<th>Common v83 wiki EXP</th>
					<th>Delta</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Snail</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Blue Snail</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Red Snail</td><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>8</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td><strong>Slime</strong></td><td>6</td><td><strong>10</strong></td><td>6</td><td class="up">+67% ⬆</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Pig</td><td>7</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Orange Mushroom</td><td>8</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Ribbon Pig</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>20</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Green Mushroom</td><td>15</td><td>26</td><td>27</td><td class="down">-4% ⬇</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Bubbling</td><td>15</td><td>26</td><td>~26</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Blue Mushroom</td><td>20</td><td>32</td><td>32</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Stirge</td><td>20</td><td>33</td><td>33</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Jr. Necki</td><td>21</td><td>38</td><td>38</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Horny Mushroom</td><td>22</td><td>35</td><td>35</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Zombie Mushroom</td><td>24</td><td>42</td><td>42</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Wild Boar</td><td>25</td><td>42</td><td>42</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Fire Boar</td><td>32</td><td>60</td><td>60</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Ligator</td><td>32</td><td>60</td><td>60</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Curse Eye</td><td>35</td><td>70</td><td>70</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Cold Eye</td><td>40</td><td>85</td><td>85</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Zombie Lupin</td><td>40</td><td>90</td><td>90</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Copper Drake</td><td>45</td><td>105</td><td>105</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Master Chronos</td><td>46</td><td>115</td><td>115</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Wraith</td><td>48</td><td>120</td><td>120</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Drake</td><td>50</td><td>135</td><td>135</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Coolie Zombie</td><td>57</td><td>190</td><td>190</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Death Teddy</td><td>85</td><td>1300</td><td>1300</td><td class="same">same</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>

		<p>
			<strong>The pattern:</strong> from Lv 15 onward, mob EXP appears
			identical to v83 within margin-of-error. The only significant
			outlier we've spotted is <strong>Slime</strong> jumping from 6 to
			10 EXP - a 67% increase that meaningfully speeds up the Lv 6-10
			bracket. Green Mushroom drops 1 EXP but that's within wiki
			variance.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Practical takeaway:</strong> if you have v83 grinding-spot
			habits, they're probably still valid EXP-per-hour. The
			<a href="/mobs">mobs catalog</a> shows the live CoT 2 datamine
			value for every mob - if you want to verify a specific number,
			search the mob and check its detail page.
		</p>

		<h2 id="quest-exp">Quest EXP: harder to compare</h2>
		<p>
			Quest EXP is much harder to A/B compare against v83 because
			community wikis rarely captured exact quest reward numbers - they
			focused on mob EXP and left "quest rewards vary" as a wave-of-the-
			hand. Datamine gives us concrete numbers. Examples pulled from
			<code>quests.json</code>:
		</p>

		<div class="table-wrap">
		<table class="exp-table">
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Quest</th>
					<th>Min Level</th>
					<th>CoT 2 EXP reward</th>
					<th>Region</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Mai's Training</td><td>3</td><td>70</td><td>Maple Island</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>

		<p>
			The comparison table is intentionally small - we're avoiding
			citing v83 quest EXP numbers we can't confidently source. The
			<a href="/quests">quests catalog</a> lists every CoT 2 quest with
			its EXP + item rewards; if a value looks different from your
			memory, treat the datamine number as authoritative for CoT 2.
		</p>

		<h2 id="party-formula">Party EXP formula: the big unknown</h2>
		<p>
			The v83 canonical party EXP formula is:
		</p>
		<pre class="callout formula"><code>partyExp(member) = mobBaseExp * (0.6 / partySize + 0.4 * (memberLv / totalPartyLv))</code></pre>
		<p>
			Our <a href="/guides/party-exp">Party EXP guide</a> uses this
			formula as its working assumption because it's the most-cited
			v83 formula and Nexon has not publicly announced retuning the
			party split. But <strong>we do not have datamine-level
			confirmation that CoT 2 preserves this exact formula.</strong>
			Reasons to be cautious:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Party formulas typically live in server code, not
				client files.</strong> Datamining the client doesn't
				surface the party-split math the way it does mob EXP.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Nexon has retuned party bonuses in other MapleStory
				builds.</strong> Modern KMS and BB-era GMS both use
				materially different party formulas from classic v83.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>New systems interact with party EXP.</strong>
				Citizenship contribution may bundle into party turnins; the
				new Physical Fitness Test event system likely has its own
				reward structure entirely.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			<strong>What to do about it:</strong> assume the v83 formula is
			directionally correct (partying benefits both members, 3-player
			parties benefit modestly more) but don't build a spreadsheet
			around exact percentages. Once Founder's Access opens October 6
			we'll do controlled tests and publish confirmed numbers on the
			<a href="/guides/party-exp">Party EXP guide</a>.
		</p>

		<h2 id="what-nexon-can-tune">What Nexon typically retunes vs leaves alone</h2>
		<p>
			Historical pattern across MapleStory relaunches (KMS Reboot, BB,
			MSEA "Renegades"): Nexon rarely touches individual mob EXP but
			frequently touches:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Level curve thresholds</strong> (EXP-to-level values
				at each level). This is the big multiplier - even without
				touching mob EXP, tuning the level curve makes the same mob
				feel faster or slower.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Party bonus multipliers</strong> and buff bonuses
				(Holy Symbol, Rainbow Mark, etc.).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Event EXP multipliers</strong> (2x events, weekend
				bonuses, first-week launch bonuses) - these are the biggest
				live-server variables and don't appear in datamine at all.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Quest reward EXP</strong> at the individual quest
				level, especially for chain quests where a single retune
				changes leveling meta.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			What Nexon <strong>rarely</strong> touches:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Individual mob base EXP - consistent with what we see in the
				datamine (mostly unchanged).
			</li>
			<li>
				Class base kits at 1st job (Bowman is a notable exception this
				time - see <a href="/guides/skill-changes">Skill Changes</a>).
			</li>
			<li>
				Base stat formulas (STR-to-damage, DEX-to-accuracy, etc.).
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="how-to-fact-check">How to fact-check any EXP claim</h2>
		<p>
			Two workflows depending on what you're checking:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Specific mob EXP:</strong> search the mob on
				<a href="/mobs">the mobs catalog</a>, open its detail page,
				read <code>stats.exp</code>. That's the CoT 2 datamine value
				- authoritative for CoT 2, ignore whatever the v83 wiki says.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Specific quest EXP:</strong> search the quest on
				<a href="/quests">the quests catalog</a>. Reward EXP is
				listed at the top of the detail page.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Level curve / party formula:</strong> we don't yet
				have these live-verified for CoT 2. Wait for Founder's Access
				data or run your own controlled test post-launch.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Event multipliers:</strong> check Nexon's official
				MSCW announcements. These change weekly and are never in
				datamine.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="bottom-line">Bottom line for returners</h2>
		<p>
			Your v83 grinding-spot instincts are mostly still valid. Mob EXP
			for iconic mobs (Wild Boar, Cold Eye, Wraith, Drake, Death Teddy)
			matches the wiki you remember. Where things get uncertain is:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Party bonus percentages</strong> - directionally
				correct, exact values TBD.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Quest reward EXP</strong> - CoT 2 datamine is
				authoritative; treat old wiki quest-EXP numbers as vibes.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Level curve</strong> - unclear. If leveling feels
				faster/slower than v83 memory, it's probably the curve rather
				than mob EXP.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Post-launch event multipliers</strong> - completely
				unknown until Nexon announces the event schedule.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<p class="closing">
			Related: <a href="/guides/whats-new-in-cot2">What's New in CoT 2</a>
			for the broader v83-vs-CoT2 changelog,
			<a href="/guides/skill-changes">Skill Changes Per Class</a> for
			the class-kit-level diffs, and <a href="/guides/party-exp">Party
			EXP &amp; Grinding</a> for the (v83-assumed) party formula math.
		</p>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.section-heading {
		max-width: 68ch;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
	}

	.prose {
		max-width: 76ch;
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.62;
		color: var(--text-body);
	}
	.prose h2 {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose p,
	.prose ul,
	.prose ol {
		margin-top: 0.7rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
	}
	.prose li {
		margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose code {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.88em;
		background: var(--bg-cloud);
		padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
		border-radius: 0.25rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
	}
	.prose a {
		color: var(--accent-primary);
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-thickness: 0.09em;
		text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
	}
	.prose strong {
		color: var(--text-heading);
	}
	.prose .closing {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
		padding-top: 1rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		font-style: italic;
		color: var(--text-muted);
	}

	.callout {
		margin: 1.2rem 0;
		padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
		border-radius: 0.5rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		background: var(--bg-cloud);
		border-left: 4px solid var(--accent-primary);
	}
	.callout--framing {
		font-size: 0.94rem;
	}
	.callout.formula {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.88rem;
		overflow-x: auto;
	}
	.callout.formula code {
		white-space: pre;
		background: transparent;
		padding: 0;
		border: none;
	}

	.table-wrap {
		overflow-x: auto;
		margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
	}
	.exp-table {
		width: 100%;
		border-collapse: collapse;
		font-size: 0.92rem;
	}
	.exp-table th,
	.exp-table td {
		padding: 0.45rem 0.7rem;
		text-align: left;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-card);
	}
	.exp-table th {
		background: var(--bg-cloud);
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-weight: 700;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.05em;
	}
	.exp-table td.same {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: 0.86rem;
	}
	.exp-table td.up {
		color: #158a4d;
		font-weight: 700;
	}
	.exp-table td.down {
		color: #b03b3b;
		font-weight: 700;
	}
	.exp-table tbody tr:hover {
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 5%, transparent);
	}
</style>
